  "page1": {
    "title": "Splitting Things into Tiny Parts",
    "subtitle": "How dividing decimals helps us share fairly, down to the smallest part.",
    "body": "Imagine you must share a ribbon fairly. Sometimes the pieces are not whole numbers. A decimal helps us count parts smaller than one. The dot in a number shows where the whole part ends. After the dot, each place stands for a tiny part. Dividing decimals is like sharing things into tiny parts. Suppose you have 7.5 meters of ribbon for three friends. You ask how much each friend gets. 7.5 divided by 3 is 2.5, so each one gets 2.5 meters. The trick is to <mark>line up the dot</mark> in the right place. If you forget the dot, your answer grows ten times too big. So 2.5 is correct, but 25 is wrong. We can check it because 2.5 times 3 equals 7.5. Decimals let us share with care.",
    "illustration": "assets/illustrations/venus/2026-07/03.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "A 7.5-meter ribbon split evenly into three 2.5-meter pieces, with the decimal dot lined up.",
    "glossary": [
      {
        "term": "decimal",
        "ko": "소수 (1보다 작은 부분을 점으로 나타낸 수)"
      }
    ]
  },
